WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.540
Imagine an ai that knows your favorite jokes, weird rabbit holes, and daily habits, then turns them into art. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 149. A Reddit user shared 30 ready-made prompts that make ChatGPT pull details straight from its memory of your past chats. Instead of starting from a blank page, you let the tool reach back into everything you have already told it about your hobbies, pets, inside jokes, and daily routines.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:59.520
Think of it like handing a sketch artist a scrapbook you have been filling for months, rather than trying to describe yourself in one rushed sentence. The prompts turn that memory into images, such as a cutaway house labeled Inside My Brain, with separate rooms for work, hobbies, and running jokes. Another prompt creates a video game character select screen, where each playable character shows a different side of your personality. A third turns your habits into a board game, with spaces that are

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:24.820
used and rules drawn from what you actually do every day. Most image tools fall back on broad stereotypes that could fit anyone, but these prompts aim for results that feel specific to you. For students working on projects about identity or memory, the approach gives a fresh way to explore self-expression without starting from scratch. You might be surprised when the image includes a small detail you had forgotten you ever mentioned.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:53.540
The prompts work best after you have used chat GPT for a while, with memory turned on, so the notebook of details has time to grow thick. To try it, open the chat GPT site on your phone or laptop, copy one of the prompts from the original post, paste it in, and add a note telling the tool to use everything it already knows about you. If you want the person in the picture to look like you, upload a reference photo and ask the tool to keep your recognizable features.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:02:20.000
The results can feel both fun and oddly revealing at the same time. Here is the deeper part worth understanding. Picture the chatbot as a student who keeps a running notebook of every conversation you have ever had. Each new message adds a few lines to that notebook about your tastes, routines, and stories. When you ask for a personal image, the tool flips through the notebook and pulls out the matching entries instead of guessing from nothing.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:35.020
The longer you chat over weeks, the more pages the notebook contains, which is why later images feel more accurate. It is not reading your mind or storing secrets. It is simply treating your past messages like a reference sheet it keeps on the desk.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:47.740
Once you picture that notebook, the feeling that the AI somehow knows you starts to make practical sense. That same notebook habit is also why you need to stay careful about what you type into personal accounts.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:03:06.880
A recent warning highlights the risks of feeding work or school data into free versions of tools like chat GPT without approval. When you paste internal documents or customer details, that information can be stored or reviewed by the AI company in ways your employer or school never agreed to.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:15.180
Students with part-time jobs should check the rules first before dropping any real names, numbers, or private files into a personal chat.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:23.320
The practice is sometimes called shadow AI because people use the tools quietly and do not always see the privacy ripple effects.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:35.200
You can sidestep the problem by keeping work and personal conversations in completely separate accounts. If you are unsure, start with general brainstorming that never includes actual private details.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:49.480
In other news, a judge ruled that the Pentagon could not blacklist the AI company anthropic on the earlier grounds given. The decision shows that governments and AI labs are still working out the basic rules of how they will treat each other.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:04:02.960
It is a reminder that policies around these tools are being written and rewritten in real time. That court outcome also shows why paying attention to the legal side matters even if you are just using the tools for school projects.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:10.260
One quick experiment you can run today is to open the chat GPT site and try the board game prompt first.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:17.560
Paste the prompt, add the instruction to pull from your memory, and notice which small details the image actually includes.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:25.080
Another experiment is to create the character select screen version and compare it to how you usually describe yourself to new people.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:31.320
Both take only a minute and give you a concrete sense of what the memory feature can and cannot capture.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:38.400
A third quick try is to test the code of arms prompt and see which symbols feel most personal once the image appears.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.200
Each one costs nothing and works in a regular browser.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.360
The court ruling on anthropic is worth watching because it affects how quickly new tools reach the public.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:55.260
For now, it mainly affects the companies themselves, but the pattern will shape what free tools look like in the coming months.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:01.100
